course: dictionary
lesson: 1122
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word break has many meanings. You can find the right one by reading the words around it. «The TV broke, so we could not watch the film.» The sentence says the TV stopped working, so broke means it stopped working, not that it was smashed.
explain: «Kintsugi is a Japanese art of mending broken pottery with gold.» Broken here means in pieces, so it can no longer be used as it was. The gold shows that the cracks can be mended and still be beautiful.
explain: Break can also be a noun, a short rest. «When something is frustrating, a short break and asking for help work better than giving up.» A break is not a broken thing here. It is time to rest before you try again.
fact: Break means to make something come apart into pieces, so it can no longer be used.
fact: Break can mean to stop working, as in «The TV broke».
fact: To break a rule or a promise means to not keep it.
fact: A break is a short rest from work or school, such as «morning break».
fact: To break a record means to do better than anyone before.
exercise: «The TV broke, so we could not watch the film.» What does broke mean here? | *It stopped working | It was turned into a picture | It became bigger and brighter | It was bought again
exercise: «Kintsugi is a Japanese art of mending broken pottery with gold.» Broken here means? | Very heavy | Shiny and new | Painted in bright colours | *In pieces, so it can no longer be used as it was
exercise: «When something is frustrating, a short break ... work better than giving up.» What is a break? | A long journey | A small piece of glass | A kind of game | *A short rest from work
test: Sam says he will break his promise to Mum. What does that mean? | *He will not keep the promise | He will write the promise down | He will say the promise twice | He will make the promise bigger
test: «Maya broke the school record for running, so she ran faster than anyone before.» What does broke a record mean? | To smash the record into pieces | To lose the record on the bus | *To do better than anyone before | To forget the record
exam: «A moving swing ... can knock you out or break teeth, so walk wide around swings.» Which meaning fits break here? | To keep a promise | *To damage something so it comes apart | To have a rest from work | To do better than anyone before
